            <ArticleTitle>[A case of &quot;postoperative erythroderma&quot; following coronary artery bypass grafting operation].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1909-13</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>So-called &quot;Postoperative Erythroderma&quot; was experienced in a 68 year-old man who received CABG for unstable angina. After a seemingly uneventful recovery, he revealed high grade fever on 13th post operative day (POD), rashes over the whole body on 15th POD and pancytopenia on 20th POD. He died of sepsis, multiple organ failure and DIC on 21st POD. Blood transfusion (concentrated red cell: 3 units) was done on operation. In this case, the rate of premature and atypical lymphocytes increased, and the ratio of OKT4 (helper)/OKT8 (suppressor) decreased. These findings of the examination suggested that there was a possibility of cell-mediated immunological depression. We considered this to be acute GVHD after blood transfusion.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
